1
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:34,700
Om namo bhagavate vasudevaya. Shri Shuka said.

2
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:54,550
One day, when the maidservants were busy with other work, Yashoda, the wife of Nanda, churned the yogurt herself. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.1]

3
00:01:01,840 --> 00:02:58,520
Before this chapter, Maharaj Parikshit asks: O brahmana, what auspicious work did Nanda do, and what did the most fortunate Yashoda do, that Hari drank the milk of her breast? [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.8.46] Devaki gave birth to Krishna in the prison, but she was helpless and had to send Him from Mathura to Gokula. Maharaj Parikshit says with regret that the father and mother themselves could not receive the happiness that Nanda and Yashoda received. To ordinary eyes this is hidden, so he asks in a covered way. Great souls often know a thing and still speak of it in the ordinary way.

4
00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:46,350
In truth the Lord was born from Yashoda's womb as well. That was explained last year. This year I will not open it again. Understand that some things are known, and still they are spoken in the ordinary way.

5
00:03:47,440 --> 00:05:01,510
Maharaj Parikshit was not satisfied with that answer. Then Shukadeva Goswami remembered the pastime in which Krishna broke the pot of yogurt and Mother Yashoda bound Him. At the end of the eighth chapter it was already said that Krishna is controlled by devotion, and that Nanda and Yashoda loved Him. Parikshit was still not satisfied. The pastime of the binding rope satisfied him fully. This chapter shows that the Lord, coming under the control of His devotee, gives a happiness that has no limit above it.

6
00:05:02,600 --> 00:07:12,140
Ekadā means one day. Yashoda, the wife of Nanda, sent the maidservants of the house to other work. There were thousands of them. She had that authority, because she was the wife of the king. This was not the daily work. A special duty had come. Preparations for the worship of Indra were going on. The cowherds worshiped Indra every year. The worship was to begin the day after Diwali, and it would continue for as many days as the Lord had held Govardhana. Kheer, puris, and many other dishes had to be made. The maids were put to that. Then Yashoda churned the yogurt herself.

7
00:07:12,140 --> 00:11:08,040
The word svayam shows that Yashoda had not been churning herself. She had the maids do it. The acharyas explain that complaints about Krishna kept coming from the houses of Braj. Krishna ate our butter, drank our milk, ate our yogurt. In truth the gopis themselves wanted Krishna to come, eat the butter, be caught, tell a lie, and give them a chance to talk with Him. Mother did not understand this. Because of vatsalya she never knew Krishna's opulence. Her worry grew: my son is not satisfied with the food at home. Other people's butter is tastier than mine. She tried in many ways. The stealing did not stop. Then her resolve became firm. She set the yogurt herself, from the milk of a padmagandha cow. It should not turn sour even a little before its time. She bathed, put on a silk lehenga and a wrap, and began to churn in a very pure way, completely absorbed.

8
00:11:09,020 --> 00:11:59,150
Nanda Maharaj was the king of the cowherds. Nine lakh cows were his own. That this only son should go to other houses, and that those people should come and tell the mother what her boy had done, was her anxiety. That is why she was churning. Shukadeva Goswami is describing how a person who cultivates vatsalya should meditate on Mother Yashoda.

9
00:11:59,150 --> 00:15:30,180
Whatever childhood pastimes had been sung here, she remembered them while churning the yogurt, and she sang them. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.2] The butter-stealing and the other childhood pastimes of Krishna in Braj, which the poets of Braj had put into songs, or which Shukadeva had spoken in Parikshit's assembly, all of these were coming back to Mother as she churned, and she was singing them. She had no other time to sing Krishna's glories. She could not sit down with a book of Vaishnava songs and begin a bhajan. Only while churning did she have the chance to sing her son's life. This remembrance was not brought in from outside. The pastimes were coming by themselves onto a heart soft with vatsalya. We cannot imagine the remembrance of one on whose lap Krishna played, against whom He slept at night, whom He called Mother, and whose breast He drank. His walking, His sulking, His speaking, His laughing, His eating, the deliverance of Putana, all of it was returning. Vishvanatha Chakravarti Thakura takes the word iha to mean, in your assembly. In Braj these pastimes were famous. Five thousand years have passed, and still when we sing, it feels as if the Lord is dancing before our eyes and stealing butter. In Braj the talk on every side was, today He did this at my house and left.

10
00:15:30,220 --> 00:17:54,390
Rupa Goswami quotes a verse in Padyavali. Krishna had lifted a small pot and was about to eat the butter. His own reflection appeared in a pillar. He was startled. He thought, there is a guard here. I have made a great mistake. He said, I have never stolen. This is the first time I have come to your house. Do not tell anyone. Do not make a noise. I will bring butter and give it to you. He brought the pot and began to feed His own reflection. The reflection could not take it. The pot began to fall. Krishna said, if you will not agree, then go and say whatever you want. Saying this, He ran. The gopi laughed. Another gopi was listening, and she laughed too. By then Krishna's feet had escaped. He was in real danger.

11
00:17:55,830 --> 00:18:50,890
Mother Yashoda was remembering and singing as she churned. As the remembrance rose in the heart, the song followed. Attention left the churning. It was like a person so deep in a task that he no longer notices whether the work is finished or whether it is time to stop. Krishna's gestures were coming back to her.

12
00:18:51,430 --> 00:23:31,960
Dressed in silk about her broad hips, a cord tied over it, her breasts wet with milk from love of her son, trembling, her eyebrows beautiful. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.3] Subhru: su means beautiful, and bhru means the eyebrow. As the childhood pastimes returned, her feelings moved with them. Sometimes she smiled. Sometimes her look was a scolding. She was churning, but her feelings were with Krishna. One who can be the mother of the most beautiful Lord must herself be that beautiful. Kshaumam vasah is a silk cloth. The women of Braj wear a lehenga below and a chunri above. To make butter for her son she wore cloth of the finest kind. The silk lehenga sat beautifully on her broad hips. Over it was a sutra, a golden waist-chain. While speaking the katha, Shukadeva Goswami is meditating on Mother. Putra-sneha-snuta-kuca-yugam. Both breasts were flowing from love of her son. The blouse was soaked. Her hands were in the churning, and she had no thought of her body. One acharya writes that the milk in her breasts was thinking, today she will take out butter so fine that Krishna will eat that and will not drink us. So it wanted to come out. Mother's milk is also beyond this world. Jata-kampam. Churning would make them move in any case, but today the trembling was special. She was taking out the butter with so much love that the milk was afraid Krishna would not drink it with the same fondness as before.

13
00:23:33,460 --> 00:24:54,540
And no other song. Tad-bala-charitani, the childhood deeds of Krishna alone. Mother was not singing any other song.

14
00:24:55,280 --> 00:27:05,020
Pulling the rope, she labored. The bangles on her arms and the earrings moved, her face was wet with perspiration, and malati flowers were falling from her hair as she churned. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.3] She was a queen. She had not churned before, and the pot was large, so the labor was real. The bangles on both arms were moving, and the earrings were moving. When the mood of a childhood pastime came, the movement of her body followed it. Drops of perspiration were on her face. It was morning, and still there was labor. A garland of malati was in her hair, and the flowers began to fall. She had no awareness of her ornaments, her body, or the labor. Service of the Lord should have this absorption.

15
00:27:41,980 --> 00:29:26,540
Mother used to wake long before, but Krishna would still be asleep, so she stayed there. The moment He woke He climbed into her lap and began to drink. Today, when sleep left Him, Mother was not there. She was churning yogurt. In the mood of a child, from great affection for His mother, He began to cry as if He were crying from hunger. Small children act like this. Where has she gone, where has she gone. Mother did not notice. He set off toward the sound of the churning. What is difficult for the all-knowing Lord? He understood that Mother was completely absorbed.

16
00:29:28,720 --> 00:34:10,950
Krishna, wanting her breast milk, came to His mother as she was churning, caught the churning rod, and stopped her, bringing her delight. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.4] See Krishna's cleverness. How shall I draw Mother's attention? He was very small. He caught the churning rod with both hands and put a brake on it. He also used a little of His own power, because a small child's grip would not stop the churning. Mother's attention came. He was rubbing His eyes. He had just woken. Pritim avahan. By His coming, and by His catching the rod, Mother was very pleased. She was still more pleased to see that the child was slowly beginning to understand. When she stopped, Krishna put one foot on the pot, took her arm with both hands, and slowly climbed into her lap. Shukadeva says arudham. She did not lift Him into the lap. Krishna climbed Himself. For Mother this was a great happiness. There is strength in His hands, because when He applied force the churning stopped. There is intelligence too. Krishna knows that His food is in Mother's breasts. Mother did not move the blouse. Krishna moved it Himself and began to drink. Then Mother left the churning. One devotee comments that the scriptures too are churned in order to reach Him. When I have come, what churning remains? This was not the churning of scripture. It was Mother's service. Later the churning did not merely stop. The pot broke as well. The whole work came to a halt.

17
00:34:11,080 --> 00:39:21,510
The Lord came to Mother Himself. Mother did not go to Krishna, because she was full of love. Every day she lifted Him from the bed. Today He walked there Himself. Sridhara Swami, the great commentator on the Bhagavatam, says the Lord is teaching this. A person may be engaged in the work of the body or the family, according to his own nature. If even then he sings the Lord's pastimes with love, the Lord will leave His own work and come to him. The Lord was asleep. He left sleep and came. The principal practice is singing the pastimes. The housework may go on, but there should be song. Mother was not making a resolve to call Krishna. She was working in a mother's affection and singing her son's pastimes. The Lord left everything and came. In Srimad-Bhagavatam, especially in the Tenth Canto, whenever someone attained Him, it was upon a song. The gopis sang on the bank of the Yamuna, and the Lord appeared in the rasa. The gopis who began to weep and sing from the longing to meet Krishna, He came to them. Yashoda was singing, and the Lord came to her. If someone has worshiped greatly before, and for some reason that worship has stopped, the Lord remembers the earlier worship and comes to that devotee. Muchukunda Maharaj was asleep in a cave, in the mode of ignorance. The Lord ran to that sleeping devotee, bringing Kalayavana with Him. With His own mouth He said, you have worshiped Me greatly, you wanted to see Me, and remembering that I have come. At that time Muchukunda was not doing bhajan. Kalayavana kicked him and was burned to ash. Muchukunda drew the sheet over himself and went back to sleep. That was not a state of bhajan. There are other places too where the Lord comes when He is called and when He is sung. In the practice we have been taught, singing is the principal thing. Arati is done while singing. While walking, there is japa. There is also reading. Katha too is singing. It is a telling. Mother was singing, and then Krishna came. This is the indication of the practice.

18
00:39:22,470 --> 00:40:56,420
Here the word Hari is not only Krishna's name. Shukadeva is translating Mother's feeling. Krishna knows, I was born from her womb, she is My mother. If I stop her, she will not scold Me. She will feed Me. She will not say, let me finish this work, then come. That is how much vatsalya there is. Krishna is sure that if He stops her, she will stop. Mother is seeing Him as Hari. The crying face, the catching of the rod, the climbing into the lap, these are pastimes that win the heart. Her mind is wholly taken up with seeing Krishna, kissing Him, and feeding Him milk. That is why the word Hari is used. Krishna is stealing Mother's mind. Even an ordinary child, doing this, sends a stream of joy through a mother's heart. What then, when the Supreme Himself is playing as the son.

19
00:40:58,240 --> 00:43:16,700
Seated on her lap, she let Him drink the breast that was flowing from affection, and she looked at His smiling face. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.5] She is feeding the child who has climbed into her lap, and while He drinks she is looking at His face. A beautiful smile is on it. The child is in joy. He caught the rod and the churning stopped. That was one cause of her pleasure. He climbed into the lap and began to drink. He does not have to press hard with His lips, because from affection the milk is flowing by itself. It comes easily, and the taste is wonderful. So the smile spreads over His cheeks. Mother is seeing that the child is perfectly well, that He is happy as He drinks, and that He is glad of His success. Sasmitam mukham. She keeps looking at the face with its gentle smile. This is Mother Yashoda's astonishing happiness. Keep this meditation.

20
00:43:18,650 --> 00:43:59,740
Vatsalya comes before madhurya. Servitorship, friendship, and the other feelings develop inside it. Madhurya does not remain in it. It is a fully developed rasa. To feed Krishna, to give Him drink, to bathe Him, to dress Him, to see Him laughing, to kiss Him, to take Him on the lap, and even to scold Him and threaten to strike Him, all of this is the happiness of vatsalya. The Lord accepts being controlled by the devotee, and in that way He gives the devotee this happiness.

21
00:44:01,300 --> 00:46:01,400
Mother was feeding Him milk, and Krishna was drinking. The milk was not decreasing. It is the milk of Goloka Vrindavan. It does not run out. In this world a mother's milk finishes in a short time. This milk is not like that. In the First Canto of Srimad-Bhagavatam, when the Lord became Arjuna's charioteer at Kurukshetra, He was a hundred years old. When He returned to Dvaraka, milk was flowing from Mother Devaki's breasts. It was flowing from the breasts of Devaki's sisters too, the other wives of Vasudeva. The relationship is not of this world. Time does not stand in its way. Krishna is always the son, and Yashoda is always the mother. Krishna is hungry for love, so He was not satisfied either. The pastime of feeding and drinking was going on.

22
00:46:01,400 --> 00:49:29,250
He was not yet satisfied, but she put Him down and went quickly, because the milk on the stove was boiling over. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.5] Milk was set on the stove in the courtyard. That too was for Krishna. He drank cow's milk and He drank Mother's milk. What was that to the one who accepts the milk of eighty-four kos? The acharyas say the milk thought, I am performing austerity in the fire, and Krishna will be satisfied by drinking Mother's milk, so my austerity is useless. What is the use of anything that does not come into Krishna's service? Mother's eye fell on the milk rising. Krishna was so happy, drinking at her breast, and still she put Him down at once and ran. Srila Sanatana Goswami says, Krishna was very light. Why did she not take Him with her? A child can be hurt if one runs while carrying Him. So she put Him down and went. Shukadeva translates the Lord's feeling. She cannot bear even the loss of a little milk. She gave no thought to feeding Me. She went to save the milk. I left the ocean of milk, I became her son, and she leaves Me to save one or two kilos of milk. A commentator says this. In truth, protecting the things of the Lord's service is devotion. That milk was for Krishna.

23
00:49:30,630 --> 00:54:29,770
Anger arose. His reddish lips trembled, and with His teeth He bit them, and He turned on the pot of yogurt. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.6] She put Him down and went, and at this anger came. Keep this in mind from beginning to end: this is a happy pastime. The anger too happened in order to give Mother happiness. It looks as if Mother is striking Him and scolding Him, but that also is an astonishing happiness. One commentator says the anger became blessed, because it received the touch of the Lord's lips. The small red lips began to quiver. New teeth were coming in. He pressed the red lip with His teeth. That is the look of anger. Sridhara Swami says it is as if the mode of goodness is ruling the mode of passion. Anger is born of passion, so it is red. The white teeth are goodness. Be angry, but stay under control. It is as if the teeth are pressing that upon Him. Then, at once, He looked at the pot of yogurt. Mother was thinking, I can feed Him my own milk at any time. This milk of the padmagandha cow that is boiling will be spoiled. Krishna will stay hungry and then go stealing at other houses. So my milk later, and this milk saved now. Krishna does not think this out. He is a child. How the food will continue later is Mother's worry. From this much alone the anger welled up. When anger comes, a person smashes even the useful pots of his own house. It came to His mind, let me break this pot and punish Mother. All of Braj, and everything here, is for Krishna. He is breaking His own things. In anger, judgment does not remain. In this mood, the Lord too had no such judgment. A shila-putra was lying there, the son of the grinding stone, the small stone with which spices are ground. He took it and struck the bottom. A blow on the top would have made more sound, because that part was empty. Where the yogurt was filled, the sound would have been less. He had that much intelligence. With one knock a hole opened. The yogurt began to flow. Then He thought, now Mother will beat Me. The anger is spent, the pot is broken, and now He is worried about saving Himself.

24
00:54:29,770 --> 00:58:47,220
Where did He run? The same Lord who measured the whole universe in two steps could not cross the width of the spilled yogurt. He did not jump over it. He went into the next room, his feet splashing through the yogurt. The footprints came up. He is absorbed in the childhood pastime. There is no feeling, I am God. Sweetness is the essence of His godhood. Mother took the milk down and returned. Uttarya gopi sushritam payah: the gopi, having taken down the milk that was well cooked. Sanskrit is the root language. We should hold our own language firmly. Why does he say gopi? The poor woman knows that the wealth of her house is this milk and yogurt, and that her child is its heir. Then she entered the place where the yogurt was being churned. The pot of yogurt was broken. The yogurt was scattered, and her son was not where she had put Him down. It did not take her long to understand. These qualities had appeared before. She felt not the least sorrow that the pot had broken. She began to laugh. At her son's intelligence: he struck it so that there was no noise, and he had the sense not to stay. If He had been caught at that very moment, perhaps a slap would have landed. A little delay, and the anger of the other side cools. She thought, I set Him aside a little, I disregarded Him a little, and at that He became angry. He does not have the sense to know that this milk was saved for Him. He made a hole and ran. Seeing the footprints, she began to laugh.

25
00:58:47,220 --> 01:03:29,650
The footprints had come up. She went that way quietly. She became still. He must be inside. Then, from the overturned mortar, came the sound of the anklets on His feet. Krishna did not have the sense that Mother would hear this sound. The bangles were speaking, the anklets were speaking, and there was the sound of pots being taken down from the hanging basket. Mother understood. He is giving butter away in the other room. He is feeding it to the monkeys to see. If they eat it well, He will know that it is very tasty. Mother thought, let me catch Him quietly, by surprise. Now it was fixed in her mind that the habit had formed. He could throw His own mother aside. He could do anything. She took a stick in her hand. Not to strike, only to threaten. She was immersed in affection. It was from that affection that she took the stick. Until now she had not even shown Krishna a slap. Children fear a stick more. Even in studies, show a stick and the dullest child improves. She thought, I will show the stick and scold Him, and my child will become well behaved. Holding the stick close, she walked slowly. He was standing on the mortar, taking the butter kept on the hanging pot and giving it to a monkey. The singular is used for the species. He was giving it to the monkeys. His mood was, the pot is already broken, now I will not leave anything in the house. Give it away, feed it, throw it. There was fear too. Like a thief, He was looking around anxiously, in case Mother was coming. He was in anger, not wholly in fear. There was a little fear. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.8]

26
01:03:29,650 --> 01:06:04,230
Seeing her coming with a stick, He quickly got down and ran off as if afraid. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.9] Mother was coming near. Krishna had not seen her at first. Seeing the stick, He came down from the mortar and went into the lane. Then He began to run all through Gokula. Mother did not know that her child could run so fast. He was acting afraid. Children sometimes think, Mother will not hit me, and still they run. He was not truly very afraid. The full fear would come when her hand caught Him. He was running because He knew He would not get free without being bound. Mother was running too.

27
01:06:15,890 --> 01:07:23,770
The gopi ran after the one whom the minds of the yogis, even when driven by austerity, are not able to enter. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.9] The Lord whom even a yogi's mind, purified by austerity, cannot catch, a gopi is running after Him to catch Him. A yogi's pure mind quickly grasps a spiritual matter. Yoginam api sarvesham. The mind of the best of yogis becomes completely pure by austerity and devotion, and then it grasps the Lord's form and qualities. [Bhagavad-gita 6.47] Shukadeva says that even the mind of an ordinary yogi, purified by austerity, cannot catch the Lord.

28
01:07:24,340 --> 01:11:20,030
When Mother caught His hand, He began to cry in sobs. I am coming, I am coming. Very loudly. Crying is a child's only strength. The crying of a woman and of a child is counted as strength. They do not fight. By crying they get a great deal done. Time and Yamaraj are afraid of the Lord. He is afraid of Mother. Brahma and Shiva praise Him, and Mother Yashoda is scolding Him and rebuking Him. I was taking out butter for You. From today I will not give You butter. I will not let You play with Your friends. Your independent behavior has gone on long enough. Mother was not in anger. It was for His good. The child should be benefited. The Lord became completely afraid. Kunti is the witness. The gopi took up the rope when You had committed an offense, and Your state, eyes disturbed, tears washing the kajal, bewilders me. Fear itself is afraid of You. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 1.8.31] Kunti was in Braj that day and saw this pastime with her own eyes. She says, when the gopi lifted the rope, Your condition was not acting. You were truly afraid. I remember that pastime. As Mother ran to catch Him, the flowers of her hair were falling, perspiration was breaking out, and because of her broad hips she was tired. She was running after Krishna again and again. The Lord was thinking, she is My mother, but a mother will strike too, for one's good. Earlier He had thought, I will run a little, and when she catches Me she will not be very angry. Now He was convinced that He had troubled her too much. At last the gopi caught Him with a snatch.

29
01:11:20,420 --> 01:14:34,310
Tears from the eyes. Mother caught the right hand. With the back of the left hand He began to rub His eyes. The tears were washing the kajal. Mother saw that the boy had gone wrong. She was showing mercy in another way. He was crying hard. When Mother understood that the child was completely afraid, that He thought she would strike Him, she began the work of binding Him. Strike or bind, the trouble remained the same. The binding went on. How she bound Him, I will tell in the next sitting. This pastime enters the hearts of the devotees. By these pastimes people can worship Krishna and be drawn to the Lord. She has put the stick down, but now she will begin to bind Him. She thought, He is very frightened. He may become unhappy and run into the forest. Mahavana was forest upon forest. In a sudden huff He will run into the forest. That is why she wants to bind Him. When children are tied and kept, they cry even more. Balarama did not come while the binding was going on. He will come later. The gopis will come too. The binding took a long time. He was not bound quickly. That pastime comes later.

30
01:14:38,080 --> 01:22:37,560
Om namo bhagavate vasudevaya. Catching that offender, who was crying and rubbing His two eyes with His own hand so that the black ointment spread, His eyes distressed with fear, she held His hand and threatened Him. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.11] She caught Him with a snatch, and Krishna began to cry very loudly. Prarudantam. Why did Mother catch Him? He was running so hard, she should have let Him go. Kritagasam. He had committed offenses. He broke the pot. He was giving butter to the monkeys. He tired Mother by running through the lanes. A child's offense should be checked, so that he becomes a good child. Krishna too was feeling that He had committed an offense. Earlier He did not believe she would strike Him. He had thought, I will run a little and she will laugh and let Me go. When she caught Him by force, He became certain that now she would strike too. The moment He was caught, He began to cry loudly. The kajal began to spread. Svapanina, with His own hand. This is the same Krishna whose tears Mother used to wipe with her own hand when He cried. Today Mother is ready to strike, so His own hand is the only help. Today the poor child has become helpless. Whenever He cried, Mother at once lifted Him onto her lap and wiped the tears with the end of her cloth. That same Mother has become like an enemy today. So He is rubbing His eyes with His own hand. Kunti saw this sight with her own eyes. The ointment was spreading. In a living being's mind there is this feeling: if I want to raise mercy in someone, I look with pity filling the eyes. The Lord has become so helpless today that He is looking toward Mother with a pitiable glance. His eyes were dancing with fear. She had caught one hand, so tightly that He could not run now. He can run far from the minds of yogis. What can one gopi do to catch Him? But He cannot run far from the rope of love. He ran with His full strength, and He could not escape Mother Yashoda. She has caught the right hand. She is scolding Him. Bhishayanti means she is producing fear. She had no wish to strike. She had to threaten Him enough that from today there would be no such mischief. As she pulled, He cried more. Again and again she raised the stick. Will You break the pot again? From today I will not give You butter. I will not let You play with the other children.

31
01:22:39,540 --> 01:26:15,160
The tears were coming heavily. He was sobbing, drawing long breaths. Mother saw that He was completely frightened. She feared that in too much grief something might go wrong in the body. When a child is in great grief, his health breaks. At once she threw the stick away. She kept hold of the hand. Tyaktva yashtim. The moment she saw it, she let the stick go. She was not one who would strike. She is exceedingly full of vatsalya. Her feeling was, this is my unknowing child, he can make a mistake, I am the mother, he is dear to me, I do not want to strike. As soon as the stick was thrown, Krishna took a little courage. The crying lessened a little. When she lifted the rope, to bind Him, the crying began again. In this world the punishments are striking and binding. The Lord is not being struck, but He will be bound, and the binding will be to a dull stone. Stay there. No playing, no eating, no talking. The lap is out of the question. She has taken Him off the lap. You have been so wild, now go to the lap of the mortar. The desire to bind Him arose. First the rope of her hair came to hand. A silk cord of about one or two meters, tied in the hair and hanging on her body. She tied one end to the mortar and began to wrap the other end around His waist. Offenders too are bound at the waist, and the hand was already caught. As she went to make the knot, the rope fell short by two fingers. Shukadeva first describes the binding itself.

32
01:26:28,440 --> 01:27:58,670
Maharaj Parikshit is, as it were, asking. While drinking milk He showed the universal form twice. He killed Trinavarta. He killed Shakatashura. Did Yashoda not know that this is the Supreme Lord? Why did she have no knowledge of the Lord's power, that she began trying to bind Him? Shukadeva says, atad-virya-kovida. She was not expert in Krishna's prowess. The affection was so thick that in the flood of that affection the memory of His opulence did not remain. The love in her heart drove the opulence and the prowess far away. She had seen the universe in His mouth, and she was taking even that as a bad dream. Who knows what you are seeing in His mouth. Some ghost, some spirit, some tantric has done something. In the end she does not consider Krishna to be the Lord. Therefore she tried to bind Him, and she bound Him.

33
01:27:58,670 --> 01:30:51,830
He has no inside and no outside, no beginning and no end. He is the beginning and the end, the outside and the inside of the universe, and He is the universe. Thinking that unmanifest, imperceptible Lord, who had appeared as a human child, to be her own son, the gopi bound Him to the mortar with a rope, as people bind an ordinary child. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.13-14] Shukadeva Goswami is happy that Krishna is being bound. So he says, Mother bound Him. Whom did she bind? The unmanifest, the one beyond the senses, the unborn. Who bound Him? A gopi. Where? To the mortar, with a rope. Mother does not say this. She considered Him her own son, and bound Him. Shukadeva says, look who was bound. The unborn, the unmanifest, the one beyond the senses, appearing in a mortal form. The binding has fallen on the Lord, on the unmanifest Brahman. Unmanifest does not mean without form. It means one who has no material manifestation. She bound Him with an ordinary rope. One who has no inside and no outside, no before and no after. Rather, He is the beginning and the end of this world, its inside and its outside, and He Himself is the world. The gopi, thinking Him her son, bound Him. There is nothing outside the Lord. A binding has to fall on the outside, and there is no outside. The rope is a small piece of this dull world. How will it wrap the Lord? The rope has no power to bind.
